### Plan-quality review
**Verdict:** FLAWED
**Reviewed:** CALP-6 — builds three delivery-quality numbers, one of which (`pass^k`) re-runs every closed ticket k times end-to-end through plan/build/QA and scores it 1 only if all k runs pass their DoD.

**Defects:**
- Design/1a commits to `pass^k` replay of every closed ticket through plan, build and QA — the Problem asked only for "a number" showing delivery quality improved — **over-scoped metric or mechanism** — expected: the plan names the machinery `pass^k` needs (a replay harness that can drive a ticket from brief to merge without a human, a grader that runs each ticket's DoD, and k x full build cost per ticket), none of which exists in this repo (`grep -rni replay` finds no harness — only `tools/adt_backfill_cost.py`'s unrelated session replay), and either scopes it as its own ticket or drops it. As written it is one bullet of three and larger than the other two combined.
- "Why this approach: the eval set costs nothing because the tickets exist" — the Problem asked for a measured baseline, not a cheap one — **over-scoped metric** (the cost claim is the load-bearing justification and is only half true) — expected: the eval *set* is free; the *runner* and *grader* are the ticket. Say which is being costed.
- Sub-steps 1b (follow-on rate from `follow_ons:`) and 1c (post-merge defect count from bugs naming a closed ticket) are listed as things to build — the Problem asked for a number, and both numbers already exist — **re-implements what exists** — expected: `tools/adt_metrics.py` already parses `follow_ons:` frontmatter (`FOLLOW_ONS_RE`, line 45) and already computes post-merge defects (`post_merge_defects()`, reported as "THE COUNTER-METRIC"), with tests at `tools/tests/test_adt_metrics.py`. The plan should cite that tool and scope 1b/1c as "run it / extend it", not as new work.
- No simpler alternative is stated anywhere in the Design — working-style #2 requires the one-line version and what specifically breaks it — **complexity nothing forces** — expected: name the simple version (run the existing `adt_metrics.py` over the closed set and report the trend) and say what about it fails to answer the PO, before reaching for replay.

**Checked and found sound:** (1) the follow-on/defect pairing is a real anti-Goodhart design, not a decorative second number — `adt_metrics.py` makes the same argument, so metric 3 genuinely earns its place beside metric 2; (2) `pass^k` over `pass@k` is the right choice of estimator for a reliability claim and the plan says why, so the *statistic* is not the defect — its cost is; (3) the Risks section does disclose that replay cost scales with k, which is a recorded carve-out and not itself a finding.

**Notes:** The cost risk is disclosed; the missing harness and grader are not — that omission, not the k x cost, is what makes 1a over-scoped.
